<LanguageData>

  <!-- traitAdjectives are GENDER-INVARIANT throughout, and for pt-BR that is the tightest
       constraint of any language in this mod. Odyssey's pt-BR NamerUniqueWeapon keeps English's
       word order instead of adapting it to Portuguese's postposing norm, so all three adjective
       slots PREPOSE the adjective onto a [weapon_noun] whose gender it cannot know, and our roster
       is four feminine weapons (espada longa, lança, maça, faca) against three masculine ones
       (gládio, machado, martelo de guerra). Two escape hatches that French and Spanish used are
       therefore unavailable here: a prepositional phrase ("de aletas espada longa" is broken once
       preposed) and a bare noun ("ouro ceifador"). What is left is the single legal shape, an
       adjective already invariant in gender: masculine form ending in -e (cortante, mordente,
       ardente, elegante, célebre), -l (letal, brutal, ágil, ancestral, formidável), -z (veloz,
       feroz, atroz, voraz), -ar (monomolecular, granular, irregular), -m (carmesim), or an
       invariant colour compound (vermelho sangue, preto carvão, verde jade).

       Because that shape is restrictive, these values are chosen as invariant epithets inside each
       trait's semantic field rather than as literal renderings of the English word: they are
       alternative flavour epithets for a generated name, not terminology, so substituting
       "cortante" for "honed" or "dilacerante" for "serrated" loses nothing. Each trait's label and
       description stay literal. Odyssey's own pt-BR trait adjectives break invariance throughout
       (preciso, sobrecarregado, desajeitado, volumoso, monstruoso, dourado), which survives only on
       its near-all-masculine gun roster, so they are deliberately not copied even for the six ports
       whose labels and descriptions are.

       Each epithet also stays material-neutral: a universal trait rolls on wood and jade as well as
       steel, so the carbonized adjectives name the surface, never a substrate.

       Labels are noun phrases naming the physical feature (espigão perfurante, cabeça de sino,
       gavilões), which is both what the English does and what sidesteps agreement on a label that
       is displayed standalone. "gavilões" is the genuine Portuguese sword term for quillons and
       keeps the English's technical register; "zeus" stays lowercase to match Core's own
       "martelo de zeus".

       The six Odyssey ports (Cumbersome, GoldInlay, JadeInlay, Lightweight, Ornamental, Ugly) take
       Odyssey's official pt-BR labels, and its descriptions verbatim wherever our English matches
       word for word; Lightweight and Cumbersome differ only in aim-vs-swing, so just that clause is
       adapted. As in the other languages, Odyssey's Ugly adjective indices differ from ours, so they
       are re-mapped by meaning. The "Comerciantes pagarão..." sentences are Odyssey's verbatim. -->

